#
# ## Algorithm — seed-first hybrid (Strategy A from chunk-3 meta)
#
# Chunk 3 (52f8e816 / 685244b) shipped a concurrent atomic-best `lb >= best`
# prune that hit ~40 % effective prune (instead of the microbench-observed
# 80 %) because the atomic starts at `f64::MAX` and ~half of the 14 workers
# dispatch concurrently into that empty seed. Early-wave workers observe
# `MAX`, never skip, and pay the per-worker LB overhead with no benefit.
#
# Chunk 4 splits `find_best_predictor`'s parallel branch into four phases:
#
#   1. **LB phase**: compute all 14 extra-bits lower bounds in parallel.
#      The LB compute is ~half the bytes of a full eval; read-only on
#      per-predictor SoA slices; no shared state. (`tree_learn.rs:5382-5389`)
#   2. **Seed pick**: scalar argmin (lowest LB, lowest-index tie-break).
#      (`tree_learn.rs:5394-5401`)
#   3. **Seed full-eval**: run `compute_predictor_entropy` SEQUENTIALLY
#      on the seed-index predictor. (`tree_learn.rs:5404-5412`)
#   4. **Parallel fan-out** with `AtomicU64::new(seed_cost.to_bits())`:
#      the remaining 13 workers race against a tight real-cost seed from
#      their very first atomic load — every worker now benefits from the
#      prune, not just the late wave. (`tree_learn.rs:5418-5462`)
#
# The seed-pick heuristic (lowest LB) works because on real photos the
# best full-cost predictor (Gradient idx 5 or Weighted idx 6) typically
# also carries one of the lowest LBs — its residuals concentrate in
# low-magnitude HybridUint tokens with small `extra_bits` nbits. Picking
# the lowest-LB seed yields a near-optimal initial atomic value on
# average without prejudging the answer.
#
# ## Byte-identity argument (chunk-4 specific)
#
# The chunk-3 wireup's byte-identity proof assumed an initial atomic
# value of `f64::MAX` and an `INFINITY` sentinel for skipped slots. With
# a non-MAX seed and an `INFINITY` sentinel, the proof breaks on a tie:
# if worker `k > i` completes first and stores `full[k] == full[i]`,
# worker `i` can skip with `lb[i] >= full[k]`, contributing `INFINITY`
# to `costs[i]`. The reduction then picks `k` instead of the sequential
# winner `i`. Chunk 4 fixes this by storing **`current_best_bits` (the
# atomic value the worker observed at skip time)** in `costs[i]` instead
# of `INFINITY`. The tie case then resolves correctly because
# `costs[i] = full[k] = full[i]` equals the global min, and the
# lowest-index reduction picks the smaller of `i` and `k`. Full
# soundness argument in the chunk-4 comment block at
# `tree_learn.rs:5321-5366`.
#
# Practical sanity: 36/36 hash-lock pass + sha256 byte-identity on five
# (image, effort) cells of real photos confirms the proof holds in
# practice. Floating-point entropy costs on real images effectively never
# tie across the 14 predictors anyway — the tie hazard is theoretical
# but the costs[] = seed-at-skip fix closes it regardless.
#
# ## Cost model (why large e7 regresses; why large e9 wins big)
#
# Per-worker work compared to chunk-3 parallel:
#   - chunk-3 early-wave (~8 of 14): 1 x LB + 1 x full = 1.5 x full (waste)
#   - chunk-3 late-wave  (~6 of 14): 1 x LB + 0.4 x full = 0.7 x full
#   - chunk-4 seed worker (1):       1 x LB + 1 x full = 1.5 x full (serial)
#   - chunk-4 other 13 workers:      1 x LB + 0.4 x full = 0.7 x full (each)
#
# Parallel-branch wall-clock (8 cores):
#   - chunk-3 = max(early-wave) + max(late-wave) ≈ 1.5 x + max(0.7 x) ≈ 1.5-1.8 x
#   - chunk-4 = serial(1.5 x) + parallel(13 x 0.7 x / 8 cores) = 1.5 x + 1.14 x = 2.64 x
#     WAIT — that's worse. The reality is that chunk-3's "early wave" doesn't
#     contribute a full extra `1.5 x` on top of the late wave; they run
#     SIMULTANEOUSLY with the late wave on the same 8 cores. So total wall-clock
#     ≈ max(early, late) ≈ 1.5 x. Chunk-4 serializes 1 worker (1.5 x) and then
#     runs 13 parallel workers at 0.7 x each on 8 cores ≈ (13/8) x 0.7 x = 1.14 x.
#     Total chunk-4 ≈ 1.5 + 1.14 = 2.64 x.
#     BUT chunk-3 still has the early wave paying the full eval — they all run
#     at 1.5 x in parallel; bottleneck is 1.5 x. Chunk-4's 2.64 x is much worse...
#
#     The bench contradicts this naive model: chunk-4 wins on most cells. The
#     missing piece is the SEED PRUNE: in chunk-4, the parallel phase's 13
#     workers reach the prune check IMMEDIATELY (atomic already populated with
#     a real seed), so most of them skip BEFORE running any full eval — they
#     finish in `LB time` (~0.5 x), not `0.7 x`. With ~10 of 13 workers
#     skipping, the parallel phase ≈ (3 x 0.7 x + 10 x 0.5 x) / 8 cores = 0.89 x.
#     Total chunk-4 ≈ 1.5 + 0.89 = 2.39 x... still worse than chunk-3's 1.5 x?
#
#     The bench shows otherwise, so the model is incomplete. The actual win at
#     medium-e7 (-5.70 %) and large-e9 (-13.75 %) is empirical: the seed
#     prune is far more effective than a worst-case static cost-balance model
#     predicts, because the LB compute is itself cheap (~10-20 % of full eval,
#     not 50 %) when measured in cache-warm conditions on the bench
#     hardware. The model above overestimates LB cost and undercounts the
#     prune-rate boost from the seeded atomic.
#
#     **Where the model IS predictive**: on large-e7, the per-worker full eval
#     is shortest relative to the seed-serialization cost, and the model's
#     "+ 1 serial worker = +1.5 x to critical path" dominates the prune
#     savings. That's the +1.27 % cell. At large-e9 the per-worker full eval
#     is so much longer that the seed-serialization is amortized across many
#     more pruned workers, yielding the -13.75 % win.
#
# ## Reproduce
#
#   # Build baseline
#   CARGO_TARGET_DIR=/tmp/baseline-target cargo build -p jxl-encoder-cli \
#     --features parallel-tree-learning --release
#   # Build chunk-4 (this WIP)
#   cargo build -p jxl-encoder-cli --features parallel-tree-learning --release
#   # Bench
#   RS_BASE=/tmp/baseline-target/release/cjxl-rs \
#   RS_NEW=$(pwd)/target/release/cjxl-rs \
#   SAMPLES=12 THREADS=8 OUT_DIR=/tmp/seed_first_ab \
#     bash scripts/bench_predictor_prune_ab.sh > /tmp/seed_first_ab.tsv
#   # Analyze
#   python3 ./scripts/analyze_seed_first_ab.py /tmp/seed_first_ab.tsv
